module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.RegularSequence
public import Mathlib.Data.List.OfFn

/-!
# Positive powers of a regular sequence

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.68.9, Tag 07DV
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-regular-sequence-powers).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace RingTheory.Sequence

/-- Taking a positive power of each term preserves and reflects regularity on any module.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.68.9, Tag 07DV
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-regular-sequence-powers).

Proof (following the six steps of the DAG node).
1. By def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence, regularity is weak regularity together with
nonvanishing of the final quotient. Weak regularity of a nonempty sequence is equivalent
to its first term being a nonzerodivisor and its tail being weakly regular on the quotient
by that first term. The successive quotients are quotients by the same sum of submodules.
2. Multiplication by `f` is injective iff multiplication by `f^e` is injective for `e > 0`:
iterate injectivity in one direction, and multiply `fx = 0` by `f^(e-1)` in the other.
For a nonzerodivisor `f` and `e >= 2`, there is a short exact sequence
`0 -> N/fN -> N/f^eN -> N/f^(e-1)N -> 0`, with first map multiplication by `f^(e-1)`.
It is well defined; its injectivity follows by cancelling `f^(e-1)` from
`f^(e-1)x = f^e y`; its image is the kernel of the surjective projection.
3. Write `J = (f_i)` and `J' = (f_i^e_i)`. Since `J' <= J`, `M = J'M` implies `M = JM`.
Conversely `M = JM` implies `M = J^n M` for every positive `n`. For a nonempty sequence,
choose `n = sum (e_i - 1) + 1`. Every degree-`n` monomial in the generators has an exponent
at least `e_i`, so `J^n <= J'`, and hence `M = J'M`. For the empty sequence both ideals
are zero. Thus the final quotient is nonzero for one sequence iff it is for the other.
4. Suppose a tail is weakly regular on `N/fN`. Induct on `e` in the short exact sequence
of step 2. The tail is weakly regular on both ends, so it is on the middle term by
lem-weil-pbc-regular-sequence-short-exact-sequence, part (a).
5. Conversely suppose the tail is weakly regular on `B = N/f^eN`, with `f` a
nonzerodivisor and `e >= 2`. Put `A = N/fN`, `C = N/f^(e-1)N`, and let `K_i` be the
ideal generated by the first `i` tail terms. Induct on `i`, keeping weak regularity of
this prefix on `A` and exactness of `0 -> A/K_i A -> B/K_i B -> C/K_i C -> 0`.
The base case is step 2. Injectivity on the middle term implies injectivity on the
submodule on the left, so the next prefix is weakly regular on `A`. By step 4 with
exponent `e-1`, it is also weakly regular on `C`. Apply
lem-weil-pbc-snake-lemma-modules to multiplication by the next term on this exact row:
the right kernel vanishes, so the induced row of cokernels is again short exact.
These cokernels are the quotients by `K_(i+1)`, proving the induction. This proves weak
regularity on `A`; the case `e = 1` is immediate.
6. Induct on sequence length, with the empty case vacuous for weak regularity.
Use step 1 to pass to the tail on `M/f_1 M`, apply the length induction to power the
tail, then steps 4 and 5 to power the first term. Step 2 identifies injectivity of the
first term and its power. This proves equivalence of weak regularity. Combine with
step 3 and the characterization in step 1 to obtain equivalence of regularity. -/
theorem isRegular_ofFn_pow_iff {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] {r : ℕ}
    (f : Fin r → R) (e : Fin r → ℕ) (he : ∀ i : Fin r, 0 < e i) :
    IsRegular M (List.ofFn f) ↔ IsRegular M (List.ofFn (fun i ↦ f i ^ e i)) := by
  sorry

end RingTheory.Sequence
